231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (1. The repeat is sidereal, and not exact)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (Agnew and Larson \(GPS Solutions 11\(1\), 2007\): the constellation repeats once per sidereal day, the revolution period is half of one, and the topocentric repeat averages 247 s short. MEO ground tracks repeat by design: GPS 2 revs per sidereal day, Galileo 17 per 10, BeiDou 13 per 7 \(ESA Navipedia\).)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (2. Harmonics of the orbital period, confirmed)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
234 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (Senior, Ray and Beard \(GPS Solutions 12\(3\), 2008\) found periodic signals in every GPS clock type at harmonics n = 1 to 4 of an 11.9826 h fundamental, caused by the J2 relativistic effect and radial orbit error. In the Earth-fixed frame that tone splits: x and y show 7.98 h and 23.93 h, z keeps the period.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (3. The standard toolkit, and its ceiling)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
236 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (Polynomial, grey GM\(1,1\), Kalman and ARIMA are the classical clock-bias models \(Remote Sensing 17:3177, 2025\). All lose accuracy as the horizon extends, and ARIMA assumes stationary data. A 7-day window with 26-hour gaps is not.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
237 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (4. Learned models, and what they give here)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
238 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (LSTM clock-bias prediction \(GPS Solutions, 2021\) and ML with explicit periodic terms \(Scientific Reports, 2025\) report real gains. We reproduced the class: six regressors, 400 windows, 27,000 rows. All stall at the 7-day mean, 0.452. Ours is 0.379.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
