349 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (The Gap)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
350 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (GPS & Galileo broadcast a live "trust bound" on each satellite's error, aviation-grade, fault-flagged in seconds. NavIC has no such validated real-time layer \(only post-hoc research checks; GAGAN covers GPS, not NavIC\).)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
351 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (Their own caveat. SISMA is required for the final integrity-risk figure, and meeting the baseline concept's required performance remains challenging. Mature, not flawless.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
352 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (NavIC's ground segment estimates and predicts satellite position and computes integrity, ionospheric and clock corrections, and receivers use the broadcast ionospheric correction. It also transmits a health and accuracy parameter analogous to SISA: the integrity check uses the User Range Accuracy upper bound, the fit interval and the health flags in the navigation message. It is not a vacuum.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
353 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (What our model adds)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (Bottom line. NavIC has the same class of ground corrections and does broadcast an accuracy and health parameter. What is missing is a NavIC-native, aviation-certified, real-time integrity system on par with SISA and SISMA plus EGNOS.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (That gap is what this method fills, and it will work on NavIC)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (Our forecast is derived from orbital mechanics and fitted inside the window it is handed. No weights file, no stored training set, nothing tuned to GPS. Moving to NavIC means supplying its orbital periods, GEO and inclined GSO, and the same closed-form beat frequencies follow. On NavIC broadcast-versus-precise error it yields a predicted day-ahead error with a calibrated 1-sigma per channel: a forward-looking bound in the spirit of SISA, computed per satellite from its own record rather than waiting on a ground-segment upload.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
